    card-drafting-civilization
      id = card-drafting-civilization
      title = Card-Drafting Civilization
      category = Strategy & Tactics
      status = Complete
      date = 2026-09-01
      tagline = Simultaneous pick-and-pass drafting across three ages into a tableau of resources, military, science, and wonders.
      summary = Three ages of simultaneous pick-and-pass drafting: each player takes a card from hand, passes the rest, and builds a tableau across seven wonder-driven pillars — resources, military, science, civilian structures, commerce, and guilds. No downtime at any player count, with multi-track scoring and several viable strategic routes.
      tags = drafting, civilization, tableau, engine, no-downtime
      stats
        item
          label = Ages
          value = 3
      sections
        card-drafting-core
          title = Card Drafting Core
          bullets
            Simultaneous drafting: each player picks one card from their hand, then passes the rest (no downtime)
            Multi-age structure: three ages, each with its own deck and a fixed number of draft rounds
            Pick-and-pass creates information tracking and hate-drafting (denying cards to neighbors)
        card-types-strategy-pillars
          title = Card Types / Strategy Pillars
          bullets
            Resource cards: raw materials and manufactured goods (economy/engine)
            Military cards (conflict scoring)
            Science cards (set-scoring escalation)
            Civilian structure cards (immediate victory points)
            Commercial cards (gold, discounts, trading)
            Guild cards (end-game scoring tied to neighbors)
        wonder-boards
          title = Wonder Boards
          bullets
            Each player has a unique wonder board with multiple build stages
            Stages are constructed by paying resource costs
            Stage completion grants persistent bonuses and points
            Wonder asymmetry differentiates player strategies
        economy-trading
          title = Economy & Trading
          bullets
            Gold income, spending, and a discard-for-gold conversion
            Resource trading with adjacent neighbors at set prices
            Commercial structures reduce trade costs / grant discounts
        conflict-system
          title = Conflict System
          bullets
            Military card totals compared against neighbors at each age's end
            Conflict resolution awards/penalizes based on relative strength
            Three escalating conflict rounds across the game
        science-scoring
          title = Science Scoring
          bullets
            Science symbol accumulation scores via set completion (escalating curve)
            Tradeoff between spreading symbols vs. concentrating
        simultaneous-play-scale
          title = Simultaneous Play & Scale
          bullets
            Full simultaneity: all players act at once each turn
            Scales 3–7 players (draft flow adjusts at low counts)
            Player interaction is largely indirect (drafting, military adjacency, guilds)
        scoring-victory
          title = Scoring & Victory
          bullets
            Multi-track final scoring: military, science, civilian, guilds, wonder points, treasury
            Multiple viable strategic routes
            Fast, low-down-time pacing
        expansion-layers-optional
          title = Expansion Layers (optional)
          bullets
            Add leader drafting, city cards, new wonders, and modular rule changes without replacing the core draft
    worker-placement-farming
      id = worker-placement-farming
      title = Worker-Placement Farming
      category = Strategy & Tactics
      status = Complete
      date = 2026-09-01
      tagline = Tight worker-placement farming where feeding pressure and card-driven specialization shape every choice.
      summary = A tight worker-placement farming economy: a limited family of workers claims scarce action spaces each round, sowing fields and raising animals against a relentless feeding pressure that punishes overreach. Occupations and improvements shape specialization, and a large end-game scoring matrix rewards a balanced farm.
      tags = worker-placement, farming, economy, feeding, euro
      stats
        item
          label = Rounds
          value = 14
      sections
        worker-placement-core
          title = Worker Placement Core
          bullets
            Limited family members (workers); placing one claims an action space for the turn
            Most action spaces allow only one worker (direct blocking/competition)
            New action spaces unlock progressively as the game's round track advances
            Family growth: expanding your worker pool (and house) is itself a strategic cost
        action-spaces
          title = Action Spaces
          bullets
            Resource gathering spaces (multiple resource types, some fixed, some escalating)
            Field plowing, crop sowing, and harvesting actions
            Animal acquisition and husbandry actions
            Building/renovation actions
            Food-gain actions
            Card-play spaces (occupations and improvements)
            Family-growth spaces
        farming-loop
          title = Farming Loop
          bullets
            Field → plow → sow → grow → harvest cycle
            Multiple crop types with planting/growth timing
            Harvest events recur across the game, tying timing to the round structure
        animal-husbandry
          title = Animal Husbandry
          bullets
            Multiple animal species with breeding mechanics (pairs breed)
            Fenced pastures and stables house animals
            Animal surplus without housing loses value
            Animal count is a scoring factor
        feeding-pressure
          title = Feeding Pressure
          bullets
            Every family member must be fed at each harvest (food cost)
            Food economy: crops, animals, ovens/hearths, food-gain actions
            Shortfalls force begging cards (negative points)
            Sustained pressure drives the whole strategy space
        cards-occupations-improvements
          title = Cards (Occupations & Improvements)
          bullets
            Personal hand of occupations (active abilities) and minor improvements (modifiers)
            Major improvements (hearths, ovens) enable food conversion chains
            Card synergy and hand management shape specialization
        house-building
          title = House & Building
          bullets
            House renovation tiers (wood → clay → stone)
            Adding rooms enables family growth
            Room count ties directly to worker count
        round-game-structure
          title = Round & Game Structure
          bullets
            Fixed 14-round track; available actions expand as rounds progress
            Multiple harvests across the game
            Game end → final scoring across a large scoring matrix
        scoring-victory
          title = Scoring & Victory
          bullets
            Points across many dimensions: fields, pastures, animals, fences, rooms, improvements, occupations, food, and cards
            Penalties for missing categories (empty spaces lose points)
            Well-rounded play vs. specialization tradeoff
        modes
          title = Modes
          bullets
            1–5 players; solo variant with its own scoring/benchmarks
            Optional card-drafting setup for fairness/variety
    asymmetric-cyberpunk-lcg
      id = asymmetric-cyberpunk-lcg
      title = Asymmetric Cyberpunk LCG
      category = Strategy & Tactics
      status = Complete
      date = 2026-09-01
      tagline = Two fully asymmetric factions duel over a click-and-credit economy of ice, runs, and agenda points.
      summary = Two fully asymmetric factions duel over a fixed click-and-credit economy: a corporation installs ice and advances agendas behind servers while a runner breaches them, stealthed or with brute force. Damage can flatline a runner, and both sides race to their own win conditions in a customizable deck-builder.
      tags = asymmetric, deck-building, lcg, cyberpunk, dueling
      stats
        item
          label = Clicks per turn
          value = 4
        item
          label = Victory points
          value = 7
      sections
        asymmetric-sides
          title = Asymmetric Sides
          bullets
            Two fundamentally different factions: a corporation that builds and defends, and a runner who hacks
            Each side has its own rules, resources, win conditions, and card pool
            True asymmetry: the Corp's board and actions differ entirely from the Runner's
        corp-side
          title = Corp Side
          bullets
            Four server types (headquarters, R&D, archives, plus remote servers)
            Install and advance cards (ice, assets, upgrades, agendas)
            Advance/score mechanic: agendas require advancement counters before scoring
            Operations: fast effects played from HQ
            Defends servers with ice protecting them
        runner-side
          title = Runner Side
          bullets
            Programs (breakers, viruses), hardware, and resources
            Runs against servers; passing ice and encountering subroutines
            Accessing cards: steal agendas or trash the Corp's stuff
            Tags and traces: being traced lets the Corp impose consequences
        ice-encounter-system
          title = Ice & Encounter System
          bullets
            Ice cards guard servers; runners must break them or suffer subroutines
            Subroutine resolution: pay to break, or eat the effects
            Ice types (barriers, sentinels, code gates) with varied counters
        click-economy
          title = Click Economy
          bullets
            Fixed clicks per turn (e.g. 4) — the core action currency
            Basic actions (gain credits, draw cards, install) always available
            Every play costs clicks, credits, or both — tight action economy
        credit-economy
          title = Credit Economy
          bullets
            Credit pool for both sides; pay for cards, installs, icebreaking, and traces
            Income sources: mandatory draw income, operations, resources
            Tempo decisions: spending now vs. building economy
        damage-system
          title = Damage System
          bullets
            Net and meat damage to the Runner's cards/hand
            Damage can flatline (eliminate) a runner with an empty hand
            Mitigation via hand size and damage prevention
        win-conditions
          title = Win Conditions
          bullets
            Corp wins by scoring agenda points OR flatlining the runner
            Runner wins by stealing agenda points OR winning by exhausting the Corp's deck
            Race to 7 points vs. alternative mill/flatline victories
        deck-construction
          title = Deck Construction
          bullets
            LCG model: customizable decks from a shared card pool
            Faction limits and influence costs shape deck identity
            Meta between the two sides drives strategic depth
